  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "There are NNN frogs, numbered from 111 to NNN.\nThere are also 2N2N2N buckets arranged in a straight line, numbered from 111 to 2N2N2N.\n\nLet AiA_iAi​ denote the index of the bucket containing frog iii. You are given the initial values of AiA_iAi​.\n\nEvery second, the following happens:\n\n\nIf all frogs are in the same bucket already, nothing happens.\nOtherwise, each frog will jump one step towards whichever is the smallest frog that's **not** in the same bucket as it.\n\nFormally, consider frog iii, and let jjj be the smallest index such that Ai≠AjA_i \\neq A_jAi​=Aj​.\nIf Aj&lt;AiA_j \\lt A_iAj​&lt;Ai​, frog iii will jump one bucket to the left and AiA_iAi​ will decrease by 111.\nOtherwise, Aj&gt;AiA_j \\gt A_iAj​&gt;Ai​, and frog iii will jump one bucket to the right, so AiA_iAi​ will increase by 111.\n\n\nNote that all movements are performed **simultaneously**. See the examples below for further clarification.\n\nYour task is to determine the largest number of frogs in a single bucket at any point of time during this process.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — the number of frogs.\nThe second line contains NNN spaced integers A1,A2,…,AN,A_1,A_2,\\ldots ,A_N,A1​,A2​,…,AN​,.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the answer: the largest number of frogs in a single bucket at any point of time.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤Ai≤2N1 \\leq A_i \\leq 2N1≤Ai​≤2N",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3\n2 1 3\n2\n2 1\n16\n2 29 6 13 26 9 30 1 25 22 5 14 10 17 18 21",
        "output": "2\n1\n8",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: The process is as follows:\n\nInitially, A=[2,1,3]A = [2, 1, 3]A=[2,1,3].\nIn the first second,\n\nFrog 111 will jump towards frog 222 which is to its left. A1A_1A1​ will decrease by 111.\nFrog 222 will jump towards frog 111 which is to its right. A2A_2A2​ will increase by 111.\nFrog 333 will jump towards frog 111 which is to its left. A3A_3A3​ will decrease by 111.\nNow, the positions are A=[1,2,2]A = [1, 2, 2]A=[1,2,2].\n\n\nIn the next second, frogs 222 and 333 will jump towards 111, while frog 111 will jump towards 222.\nThe positions will be [2,1,1][2, 1, 1][2,1,1].\nIn the third second, again frogs 222 and 333 will jump towards 111, while frog 111 will jump towards 222.\nThe positions will be [1,2,2][1, 2, 2][1,2,2].\n\nThis will continue infinitely: the frogs will alternate between buckets 111 and 222.\nThe maximum number of frogs in a bucket at any time is 222, which is frogs 222 and 333.\n**Test case 222: Frogs 111 and 222 will never be in the same bucket, so the answer is 111."
      }
    ]
  },
